f2e8c30d7a50295711a78ec67e3c2ea24d0a05aece54d17a7b9ba05"},"seconds":7.816173791885376,"seed":166781845293052076,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the expression \\(\\left(\\frac{8}{9} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^4\\right)^{-1} - 17\\), we will proceed step by step.\n\nFirst, simplify the expression inside the parentheses:\n\\[\n\\frac{8}{9} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^4\n\\]\nCalculate \\(\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^4\\):\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^4 = \\frac{1^4}{2^4} = \\frac{1}{16}\n\\]\nNow substitute \\(\\frac{1}{16}\\) back into the expression:\n\\[\n\\frac{8}{9} \\cdot \\frac{1}{16} = \\frac{8 \\cdot 1}{9 \\cdot 16} = \\frac{8}{144} = \\frac{1}{18}\n\\]\nSo, the expression inside the parentheses simplifies to \\(\\frac{1}{18}\\). Now we need to take the reciprocal of \\(\\frac{1}{18}\\):\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{1}{18}\\right)^{-1} = 18\n\\]\nNow, we need to subtract 17 from 18:\n\\[\n18 - 17 = 1\n\\]\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{1}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"prealgebra:522"}
